s we did that so a little over 30 years  ago you made this  shift from scientists mainly to  activists mainly i guess  why conference in 1986  scientific one i got my phd by then and  it was to  find out how chimp behavior differed if  it did from one environment to another  there were six study sites across africa  so we thought let's bring these  these scientists together and explore  this  which was fascinating but we also had a  session on conservation  and a session on conditions in some  captive situations like  medical research and those two sessions  were so shocking to me i went to the  conference as a scientist  and i left as an activist i didn't make  the decision something  happened inside me so you spent the last  34 years sort of tirelessly campaigning  for a better relationship between  people and nature um what  what should that relationship look like  well we you know it's again you come up  with  all these problems people have to have  space to live  but i think the problem is that we  we've become in the affluent societies  too greedy  i mean who needs honestly who needs four  houses  with huge grounds and why do we need yet  another shopping mall  and so on and so on so we are  looking at short-term economic benefit  money has become a sort of god to  worship as we lose all spiritual  connection with the natural world  and so we're looking for short-term  monetary  gain or power rather than  the health of the planet and the future  of our children  we don't need to care about that anymore  i'll never stop fighting i mean in your  work  um specifically on chimpanzee  conservation you've  um you've made it practice to put  people at the center of that local  people to engage them  how has that worked and do you think  that's an essential idea  if we're to succeed in protecting the  planet  you know after that famous conference i  thought well i must learn more about why  chimps are vanishing in africa and  what's happening to the forest  so i've got a bit of money together and  went out and visited  six range countries and learned a lot  about the  problems faced by chimps you know  hunting for bushmeat and the  live animal trade and caught in snares  and  human populations growing and needing  more land for their crops and their  cattle and their  villages but i was also learning  about the plate faced by so many people  the absolute poverty the lack of health  and education  the degradation of the land and  it came to a head when i flew over the  tiny gombi national park  it had been part of this equatorial  forest belt right across africa  to the west coast and in 1990 it was  just this little island of foresters  tiny national park  all around the hills were bare and  that's when it hit me  if we don't do something to help the  people find ways of living without  destroying their environment  we can't even try to save the chimps so  the jane goodall institute began this  program take care we call it takari  and it's our method of community-based  conservation it's  totally holistic and we've now  put the tools of conservation into the  hand of the villagers  because most tanzanian wild chimps are  not in  protected areas they're just in the  village forest preserves  and so they now go and measure the  health of their forest  they've understood now that protecting  the forest isn't just for wildlife it's  their own  future that they need the forest and  they're very proud  the volunteers go to workshops they  learn how to use smartphones  they learn how to upload into platform  on the clouds  and so it's all transparent and they've  the trees have come back  there's no bear hills anymore  they agreed to make a buffer zone around  gambi  so the chimps have more forests than  they did in 1990  and they're opening up corridors for us  to link the scattered  chimp group so that you don't get too  much inbreeding  so yes it's worked and it's in six other  countries now  same thing i mean you've been this  extraordinary  tireless voice all around the world just  traveling so much  speaking everywhere inspiring people  everywhere how  on earth do you find the  the energy you know the fire to to do  that because  that that is exhausting to do every  meeting with lots of people it is it is  just physically exhausting and yet here  you are still  still doing it how how are you doing  this jane  well i suppose you know i i'm not  obstinate i  i don't like giving up i'm not going to  let  these ceos of big companies who are  destroying the forest or the  politicians who are unraveling all the  all the protections that were put in  place by previous presidents and you  know who i'm talking about  um and you know so i'll go on fighting i  care about i'm passionate about  the the wildlife i'm passionate about  the natural world i love forests it  hurts me  to see them damaged and i care  passionately about children  and we're stealing their future and i'm  not going to give up  so i guess i'm blessed with good genes  that's a gift and the other gift which i  discovered i had  was communication whether it's writing  or speaking  and so you know if if going around like  this wasn't working  but when you every time i do a lecture  people come up and say  well i had given up but you've you've  inspired me i promise to do my bit  and we have our youth program roots and  shoots now in 65  countries and growing fars all ages  all choosing project help people animals  environment  rolling up their sleeves and taking  action and you know they  look at you with shining eyes wanting to  tell dr j  what they've been doing to make the  world a better place how can i let them  down i mean as you look at  the planet's future what worries you  most actually what what scares you  most about where we're at  well the fact that you know we have a  small window of  time i believe when we can start at  least start healing some of the harm  and slowing down climate change  but it is closing and we've seen what  happens  with the down around the world because  of kogit 19  clear skies over cities some people  breathing  clean air that they've never breathed  before and looking up at the shining  skies at night  which they've never seen properly before  and  you know so what worries me both  most is how to get enough people  people understand that they're not  taking action how to get enough people  to take action national geographic just  launched this  extraordinary film um about you uh  highlighting your work over  what six decades um it's titled  jane goodall the hope so what is the  hope  jane well the hope you know my greatest  hope is all these young people i mean in  china  people will come up and say well of  course i care about the environment i  was in roots and shoots in primary  school  and you know we have roots and shoots us  hanging on to the values  and so they're so enthusiastic once they  know the problems and they're empowered  to take action they're clearing the  streams they're removing invasive  species  humanely uh that and they have so many  ideas  and then there's you know this this  extraordinary intellect of ours  we're beginning to use it to come up  with technology  that really will help us to live in  greater harmony and in our individual  lives think of  you know let's think about the  consequences of what we do each day  what do we buy where did it come from  how was it made  did it harm the environment was it cruel  to animals is it cheap because of child  slave labor  make ethical choices which you can't do  if you're living in poverty by the way  and then finally this indomitable spirit  the people who tackle what seems  impossible  and won't give up can't give up when you  have those but you know there are things  that i can't  fight i i can't fight corruption  um i can't fight military regimes and  dictators  but so i can only do what i can do and  if we all do  the bits that we can do surely that  makes a hole that eventually will  win out so last question jane i mean if  there was one  idea one thought one seed you could  plant in the minds of  everyone watching this what would that  be  you know just remember that every day  you live  you make an impact on the planet you  can't help making an impact  and at least unless you're living in  extreme poverty  you have a choice as to what sort of  impact you make even in poverty you have  a choice but  when we are when we're more affluent we  have a greater choice  and if we all make ethical choices then  we start  moving towards a world that will be not  quite so  desperate to leave to our great  grandchildren  that's that's i think something  for everybody because a lot of people  understand what's happening but they  feel helpless and hopeless and what can  they do so they do nothing and they  become apathetic  and that is a huge danger apathy  dr jane goodall wow i really want to  thank you  for your extraordinary life for all that  you've done  and for spending this time with us now  thank you  oh thank you  you
 

